WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.072
Welcome to Module 5 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:04.072 --> 00:00:06.250
AWS Health and the support plan.

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:10.104
This is Gate 4 of the provider
notices implementation guide,

4
00:00:10.104 --> 00:00:12.725
with Chapter 4 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:12.725 --> 00:00:16.378
AWS Health publishes issues,
scheduled changes and account

6
00:00:16.378 --> 00:00:20.250
notifications for each account,
with the resources they affect.

7
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.459
The A P I is the scheduled, recorded
read of them, but it comes with a

8
00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:29.700
condition the other providers don't have:
it needs a qualifying AWS Support plan.

9
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.116
In this module you'll read a
fictional account's events,

10
00:00:33.116 --> 00:00:37.671
see which ones AWS marked action required
and which the kit's rule marked,

11
00:00:37.671 --> 00:00:40.631
build the GovCloud request
without sending it,

12
00:00:40.631 --> 00:00:44.275
and watch the kit refuse eleven
events in one details call.

13
00:00:44.275 --> 00:00:49.525
It takes about fifty minutes, with a lab
on fixtures and a lab in a test account.

14
00:00:49.525 --> 00:00:55.360
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live account.

15
00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.310
Why does AWS need its own module?

16
00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:05.330
Today, AWS sends account email
to the root user's address,

17
00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:11.273
plus up to three alternate contacts, one
each for billing, operations and security.

18
00:01:11.273 --> 00:01:15.611
The AWS Health dashboard keeps
events for up to ninety days.

19
00:01:15.611 --> 00:01:18.661
So the notices reach
whoever opened the account,

20
00:01:18.661 --> 00:01:20.873
or a page nobody opens on a schedule.

21
00:01:20.873 --> 00:01:23.859
The A P I fixes that, with a condition.

22
00:01:23.859 --> 00:01:28.007
It needs a qualifying AWS Support
plan, and without one it answers

23
00:01:28.007 --> 00:01:31.847
with a subscription required
exception, and nothing is read.

24
00:01:31.847 --> 00:01:34.935
Then the dashboard and its
email are the only path.

25
00:01:34.935 --> 00:01:38.922
And AWS's own pages don't
agree on which plans qualify.

26
00:01:38.922 --> 00:01:42.700
The AWS Health concepts page
names Business Support plus,

27
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:46.950
Enterprise Support or Unified
Operations; the A P I reference and

28
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.122
the GovCloud page name the older
Business and Enterprise plans.

29
00:01:51.122 --> 00:01:54.930
So the guide cites all three, and
tells you to check the account's

30
00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:59.240
plan against the current pages,
and to confirm it with AWS Support.

31
00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.903
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

32
00:02:05.903 --> 00:02:10.164
You'll write the reader's policy
with exactly three read actions,

33
00:02:10.164 --> 00:02:14.265
and say why not the AWS Health
full access policy, or a wildcard.

34
00:02:14.265 --> 00:02:18.628
You'll explain the support-plan
requirement, the error you get without one,

35
00:02:18.628 --> 00:02:21.703
and why to check the plan
against AWS's current pages.

36
00:02:21.703 --> 00:02:25.765
You'll map AWS Health event
categories to the record's kinds,

37
00:02:25.765 --> 00:02:30.077
keeping AWS's own actionability
field apart from the kit's rule.

38
00:02:30.077 --> 00:02:33.497
And you'll name each partition's
Health endpoint region,

39
00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:37.364
and say why GovCloud relies on
the A P I rather than EventBridge.

40
00:02:37.364 --> 00:02:41.749
Before you start, finish Module 1,
on the record and its action basis,

41
00:02:41.749 --> 00:02:45.815
and read the AWS column of the
guide's five providers compared.

42
00:02:45.815 --> 00:02:49.977
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1
or later with Pester 5.

43
00:02:49.977 --> 00:02:51.310
No account.

44
00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.285
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 4.

45
00:02:57.285 --> 00:02:59.610
The prerequisite is the support plan.

46
00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.023
Then three steps.

47
00:03:01.023 --> 00:03:03.273
One: create the read policy.

48
00:03:03.273 --> 00:03:06.949
It's a customer managed policy
with exactly three actions:

49
00:03:06.949 --> 00:03:11.622
describe events, describe event
details and describe affected entities.

50
00:03:11.622 --> 00:03:15.375
AWS publishes no read-only
managed policy for Health,

51
00:03:15.375 --> 00:03:21.173
and the AWS Health full access policy includes
writes, so the reader never holds it.

52
00:03:21.173 --> 00:03:24.841
AWS's own examples grant
describe with a wildcard,

53
00:03:24.841 --> 00:03:30.298
but a wildcard grows as AWS adds actions,
so the kit names the three it calls.

54
00:03:30.298 --> 00:03:32.798
Two: cross-check from the Health endpoint.

55
00:03:32.798 --> 00:03:38.098
Commercial accounts use u s east
one; GovCloud, u s gov west one.

56
00:03:38.098 --> 00:03:39.597
Three: collect.

57
00:03:39.597 --> 00:03:42.122
The kit reads open and upcoming events,

58
00:03:42.122 --> 00:03:46.147
then their details and affected
entities, ten events at a time.

59
00:03:46.147 --> 00:03:50.617
The gate validates when the instance
retirement comes out as a retirement,

60
00:03:50.617 --> 00:03:53.573
due at its start, with the
instance as its resource.

61
00:03:53.573 --> 00:03:56.650
The gate owner is the AWS account owner.

62
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.413
Unit 2 is the event reader.

63
00:04:02.413 --> 00:04:05.383
AWS Health's event type
categories are issue,

64
00:04:05.383 --> 00:04:09.399
scheduled change, account
notification and investigation.

65
00:04:09.399 --> 00:04:13.150
None of them says retirement or
security, so the kit maps them.

66
00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.137
An issue or an investigation
is an incident.

67
00:04:16.137 --> 00:04:20.070
A scheduled change whose event
type code names a retirement,

68
00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:24.475
a lifecycle event, a deprecation or
an end of support is a retirement.

69
00:04:24.475 --> 00:04:25.713
That's the kit's rule.

70
00:04:25.713 --> 00:04:28.313
Any other scheduled change is maintenance.

71
00:04:28.313 --> 00:04:32.238
An account notification with the
security persona is security,

72
00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:35.349
and any other account
notification is an advisory.

73
00:04:35.349 --> 00:04:36.275
Then action.

74
00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:40.001
AWS's own actionability field,
when it says action required,

75
00:04:40.001 --> 00:04:45.095
makes the record action required, with an
action basis that starts with provider,

76
00:04:45.095 --> 00:04:48.213
and a scheduled change's
start time is its due date.

77
00:04:48.213 --> 00:04:52.115
A retirement without AWS's
flag is still action required,

78
00:04:52.115 --> 00:04:55.437
by the kit's rule, due at
its start, and labeled rule.

79
00:04:55.437 --> 00:05:04.110
And AWS gives times as epoch seconds; the
record holds them in U T C, I S O 8601.

80
00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.835
Two more things the reader handles.

81
00:05:09.835 --> 00:05:11.172
First, batching.

82
00:05:11.172 --> 00:05:15.185
An event list without its description
or resources can't be routed,

83
00:05:15.185 --> 00:05:18.834
and descriptions and affected
entities are separate calls.

84
00:05:18.834 --> 00:05:22.947
Describe event details takes
one to ten event A R Ns.

85
00:05:22.947 --> 00:05:27.855
So the kit asks ten at a time, and
refuses eleven before it calls anything,

86
00:05:27.855 --> 00:05:30.684
rather than have the A
P I refuse it mid-run.

87
00:05:30.684 --> 00:05:32.360
Second, partitions.

88
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:37.584
The settings name the partition, and the
region comes from it: u s east one for

89
00:05:37.584 --> 00:05:40.364
commercial accounts, and u s gov west one,

90
00:05:40.364 --> 00:05:43.397
a single regional
endpoint, in AWS GovCloud.

91
00:05:43.397 --> 00:05:47.998
A GovCloud account read from u s east
one fails, or worse, reads nothing,

92
00:05:47.998 --> 00:05:52.760
so the kit checks once that the caller's
A R N is in the settings' partition.

93
00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.775
In GovCloud there's one more
reason to use the A P I:

94
00:05:55.775 --> 00:06:00.448
EventBridge there doesn't send public
events from the Service Health view.

95
00:06:00.448 --> 00:06:03.987
Elsewhere, the interim path
is an EventBridge rule

96
00:06:03.987 --> 00:06:07.273
on AWS Health events, which
the kit doesn't build.

97
00:06:07.273 --> 00:06:11.330
And with no support plan, it's
the dashboard and its email.

98
00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:15.805
Try it.

99
00:06:15.805 --> 00:06:21.055
Lab 1 runs in the provider notices
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

100
00:06:21.055 --> 00:06:25.732
The account is AWS's documentation
account, one one one one two

101
00:06:25.732 --> 00:06:30.592
two two two three three three
three, in the commercial partition.

102
00:06:30.592 --> 00:06:32.980
Nothing signs in or sends a request.

103
00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:35.555
Step one lists what AWS needs.

104
00:06:35.555 --> 00:06:41.317
Three calls: describe events, describe event
details and describe affected entities,

105
00:06:41.317 --> 00:06:46.430
each with its one read action, and each
verified against AWS's documentation.

106
00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.742
Three read actions, one per call.

107
00:06:48.742 --> 00:06:51.005
That's the whole policy,
and it's what you'd

108
00:06:51.005 --> 00:06:53.680
show an assessor who asks
what the reader may do.

109
00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:57.130
Step two shows each partition's
Health endpoint region.

110
00:06:57.130 --> 00:07:01.476
The commercial partition
reads from u s east one,

111
00:07:01.476 --> 00:07:04.518
with A R Ns that start A R N colon A W S.

112
00:07:04.518 --> 00:07:09.023
The GovCloud partition
reads from u s gov west one,

113
00:07:09.023 --> 00:07:13.890
with A R Ns that start A R N
colon A W S dash U S dash gov.

114
00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:21.281
Step three collects AWS
from the day-one fixtures:

115
00:07:21.281 --> 00:07:24.478
five events, four open,
three needing action.

116
00:07:24.478 --> 00:07:29.765
One incident, one maintenance window,
two retirements and one security notice.

117
00:07:29.765 --> 00:07:31.765
Step four reads the records.

118
00:07:31.765 --> 00:07:37.276
The E C 2 instance retirement is due
December first, 2026, and the Lambda

119
00:07:37.276 --> 00:07:42.978
planned lifecycle event January
fifteenth, 2027, each with its resource.

120
00:07:42.978 --> 00:07:46.252
The R D S maintenance is
scheduled on a database.

121
00:07:46.252 --> 00:07:50.240
The I A M security notification
needs action, with no date.

122
00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.165
And the S 3 operational issue is closed.

123
00:07:53.165 --> 00:07:56.303
Step five asks who said
each one needs action.

124
00:07:56.303 --> 00:08:00.330
AWS did, twice, through
its actionability field:

125
00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:04.152
the E C 2 retirement and
the I A M notification.

126
00:08:04.152 --> 00:08:08.002
The R D S maintenance and
the S 3 issue need none.

127
00:08:08.002 --> 00:08:11.295
And the Lambda event is action
required by the kit's rule:

128
00:08:11.295 --> 00:08:16.091
a scheduled change whose event type code
names a retirement, due at its start time.

129
00:08:16.091 --> 00:08:21.010
The record says rule, so nobody
mistakes it for AWS's own flag.

130
00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.285
Step six builds the GovCloud
request without sending it.

131
00:08:28.285 --> 00:08:32.572
You read the lab settings, change
the partition to A W S U S gov,

132
00:08:32.572 --> 00:08:35.110
and ask the kit for the live command.

133
00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:37.961
It's describe events in u s gov west one,

134
00:08:37.961 --> 00:08:41.548
filtered to open and
upcoming events, as JSON.

135
00:08:41.548 --> 00:08:44.472
The region came from the
partition; you never typed it.

136
00:08:44.472 --> 00:08:48.672
Step seven asks for the details
of eleven events in one call.

137
00:08:48.672 --> 00:08:51.407
The kit refuses before anything runs:

138
00:08:51.407 --> 00:08:56.347
describe event details takes one to
ten event A R Ns, and it got eleven.

139
00:08:56.347 --> 00:08:59.474
In a live run the kit never
asks for more than ten,

140
00:08:59.474 --> 00:09:04.285
so this is the guard that keeps a coding
mistake from reaching AWS mid-run.

141
00:09:04.285 --> 00:09:08.982
Step eight runs this chapter's
tests, for the AWS Health block

142
00:09:08.982 --> 00:09:13.210
and the AWS rows of the live
tests: eleven pass, none fail.

143
00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:15.410
Then delete the output folder.

144
00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.360
Lab 2 reads a real test account.

145
00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:27.676
You need an AWS test account, never
production, with a qualifying AWS Support plan,

146
00:09:27.676 --> 00:09:32.860
an I A M administrator, and an I A M
role and C L I profile for the reader.

147
00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:37.020
Step one writes the read policy
with exactly the three actions,

148
00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:40.060
creates it, and attaches
it to the reader's role.

149
00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.878
Step two checks who you're signed in as:

150
00:09:42.878 --> 00:09:48.060
the A R N must start with A R N colon
A W S, the partition the settings name.

151
00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.785
Step three cross-checks.

152
00:09:49.785 --> 00:09:53.889
Count AWS's own open and upcoming
events from u s east one,

153
00:09:53.889 --> 00:09:56.923
then collect, and the counts should agree.

154
00:09:56.923 --> 00:10:01.818
If the account has no qualifying
support plan, the subscription required

155
00:10:01.818 --> 00:10:06.553
exception is the expected result, and
that's a gap to record with the ISSO.

156
00:10:06.553 --> 00:10:09.923
Step four backs it out:
detach and delete the policy,

157
00:10:09.923 --> 00:10:13.535
and delete the run, which
names the account's resources.

158
00:10:13.535 --> 00:10:15.685
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

159
00:10:15.685 --> 00:10:19.220
The workbook's Chapter 4
has the exact commands.

160
00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:24.357
The federal requirement.

161
00:10:24.357 --> 00:10:30.364
Gate 4 supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three S A nine, part c,

162
00:10:30.364 --> 00:10:34.780
monitoring external service
providers on an ongoing basis,

163
00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:39.196
and S I five, part a, receiving
security alerts and advisories.

164
00:10:39.196 --> 00:10:44.250
For events AWS marks action required,
it also supports S I five, part d:

165
00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:48.583
implementing directives in
the established time frames,

166
00:10:48.583 --> 00:10:51.020
through the task Module 7 routes.

167
00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:56.368
The evidence is the permission list and
the policy document with its three read

168
00:10:56.368 --> 00:11:00.257
actions; the raw AWS response,
the notices and the manifest,

169
00:11:00.257 --> 00:11:05.524
read on the partition's own endpoint; and
the events AWS marked action required,

170
00:11:05.524 --> 00:11:09.170
with AWS's due date, and the
kit's rule labeled as the kit's.

171
00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:12.132
The gate's output is
evidence for these controls.

172
00:11:12.132 --> 00:11:15.053
Your organization still
sets their parameters,

173
00:11:15.053 --> 00:11:18.194
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

174
00:11:18.194 --> 00:11:22.589
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

175
00:11:22.589 --> 00:11:27.730
the agency lines its evidence up with
them, and doesn't report them itself.

176
00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:33.455
Check yourself with two questions.

177
00:11:33.455 --> 00:11:36.651
First: what does the AWS
Health A P I answer for

178
00:11:36.651 --> 00:11:40.192
an account without a
qualifying AWS Support plan?

179
00:11:40.192 --> 00:11:43.605
The answer is b, a subscription
required exception.

180
00:11:43.605 --> 00:11:47.830
Not an empty list: nothing is read
at all, and the error says why.

181
00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:50.957
Check the account's plan
against AWS's current pages,

182
00:11:50.957 --> 00:11:53.430
because they name the plans differently.

183
00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:57.267
Second: the Lambda lifecycle
event is action required.

184
00:11:57.267 --> 00:11:58.179
Who said so?

185
00:11:58.179 --> 00:12:00.443
The answer is b, the kit's rule.

186
00:12:00.443 --> 00:12:03.296
A scheduled change whose
event type code names

187
00:12:03.296 --> 00:12:06.004
a retirement is a
retirement, due at its start.

188
00:12:06.004 --> 00:12:09.543
AWS didn't set the actionability
field on that event,

189
00:12:09.543 --> 00:12:13.492
so the record's action basis
begins with rule, and states it.

190
00:12:13.492 --> 00:12:15.919
The module page has three more questions,

191
00:12:15.919 --> 00:12:18.770
each linked to the
section that answers it.

192
00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:23.345
To sum up.

193
00:12:23.345 --> 00:12:27.770
You read the reader's three actions,
saw each partition's endpoint,

194
00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:32.276
collected five events, told AWS's
actionability from the kit's rule,

195
00:12:32.276 --> 00:12:37.520
built the GovCloud request, and watched
the kit refuse eleven A R Ns in one call.

196
00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:41.785
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 4 is the raw AWS response,

197
00:12:41.785 --> 00:12:47.095
the notices as JSON and as a spreadsheet
file, and the manifest, all dated;

198
00:12:47.095 --> 00:12:51.534
the policy document; and a record
of the account's support plan,

199
00:12:51.534 --> 00:12:54.145
checked against AWS's current pages.

200
00:12:54.145 --> 00:12:58.233
An account without one is a
gap to record with the ISSO.

201
00:12:58.233 --> 00:13:01.308
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

202
00:13:01.308 --> 00:13:05.607
Next is Module 6: Google
incidents and the maintenance gap.

203
00:13:05.607 --> 00:13:08.535
Google Cloud's notices
come in only one kind,

204
00:13:08.535 --> 00:13:13.060
and that module is about writing
down honestly what doesn't arrive.
